<section class="research-scale" data-research-scale aria-labelledby="research-scale-title">
  <header class="research-scale__heading">
    <p class="research-scale__eyebrow">Materials &amp; scales</p>
    <h2 id="research-scale-title">From asteroid to microstructure</h2>
    <p>Orgueil is my research material. Bennu and Ryugu offer independent comparisons, from spacecraft observations to returned samples.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="research-scale__tabs research-scale__materials" role="tablist" aria-label="Research materials" data-material-tabs hidden>
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="research-material-tab-orgueil" aria-controls="research-material-orgueil" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">CI / Orgueil</button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="research-material-tab-bennu" aria-controls="research-material-bennu" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Bennu</button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="research-material-tab-ryugu" aria-controls="research-material-ryugu" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Ryugu</button>
    <span class="research-scale__beam" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </div>

  <div class="research-scale__materials-panels">
  <div class="research-scale__material" id="research-material-orgueil" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="research-material-tab-orgueil" tabindex="0">
    <div class="research-scale__context"><span class="research-scale__eyebrow">Research target</span><span>CI1 carbonaceous chondrite</span></div>
    <figure class="research-scale__figure">
      <div class="research-scale__media">
        <img src="/assets/img/research-scale/orgueil-smithsonian.jpg" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Orgueil meteorite specimen USNM 388: a dark, irregular fragment photographed on a wooden support in the Smithsonian collection." />
      </div>
      <figcaption class="research-scale__caption">
        <p class="research-scale__eyebrow">Orgueil / CI chondrite</p>
        <h3>A mineral record of water</h3>
        <p>Orgueil is a CI1 carbonaceous chondrite that fell in France in 1864. My research focuses on its dolomite, asking how this mineral records aqueous alteration and material evolution within a primitive parent body.</p>
        <dl class="research-scale__facts">
          <div><dt>Research question</dt><dd>What conditions controlled dolomite formation in Orgueil?</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Approach</dt><dd>Connect mineral textures, chemistry and crystal structure.</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="research-scale__credit">Specimen USNM 388. Photo: Chip Clark / Smithsonian.<br /><a href="https://naturalhistory.si.edu/object/nmnhmineralsciences_1017941">Collection &amp; CC0 image</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.mnhn.fr/fr/meteorite-d-orgueil">Meteorite record</a></p>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="research-scale__boundary">Public museum specimen, not my experimental sample. The photograph does not identify individual minerals.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="research-scale__material" id="research-material-ryugu" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="research-material-tab-ryugu" tabindex="0">
    <div class="research-scale__context"><span class="research-scale__eyebrow">Sample-return comparison</span><span>Hayabusa2 / Ryugu</span></div>
    <figure class="research-scale__figure">
      <div class="research-scale__media">
        <img src="/assets/img/research-scale/ryugu-jaxa.jpg" width="512" height="512" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Ryugu photographed by Hayabusa2's ONC-T camera on 26 June 2018: a diamond-shaped asteroid with a ridged, rocky surface against black space." />
      </div>
      <figcaption class="research-scale__caption">
        <p class="research-scale__eyebrow">Ryugu / CI-like returned material</p>
        <h3>A comparison for CI chondrites</h3>
        <p>Hayabusa2 returned samples from Ryugu to Earth in 2020. Published analyses found similarities to CI chondrites and evidence of aqueous alteration, providing a comparison for the mineral record preserved in Orgueil.</p>
        <dl class="research-scale__facts">
          <div><dt>Observation</dt><dd>Spacecraft imaging and returned-sample analysis</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Research question</dt><dd>Which alteration signatures are shared by CI meteorites and returned asteroid samples?</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="research-scale__credit">Image: JAXA, University of Tokyo and collaborators.<br /><a href="https://global.jaxa.jp/press/2018/06/20180627_hayabusa2.html">JAXA image</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.isas.jaxa.jp/en/topics/003094.html">Sample analysis</a> &middot; <a href="https://global.jaxa.jp/policy.html">Media use</a></p>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="research-scale__boundary">Public mission image and published findings, not my experimental results. Similarity to CI chondrites does not establish Ryugu as Orgueil's parent body.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="research-scale__material" id="research-material-bennu" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="research-material-tab-bennu" tabindex="0">
  <div class="research-scale__tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Bennu observation scales" data-scale-tabs hidden>
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="research-scale-tab-asteroid" aria-controls="research-scale-panel-asteroid" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">
      <span class="research-scale__number" aria-hidden="true">01</span> Asteroid
    </button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="research-scale-tab-sample" aria-controls="research-scale-panel-sample" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">
      <span class="research-scale__number" aria-hidden="true">02</span> Sample
    </button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="research-scale-tab-microstructure" aria-controls="research-scale-panel-microstructure" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">
      <span class="research-scale__number" aria-hidden="true">03</span> Grains
    </button>
    <span class="research-scale__beam" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </div>

  <div class="research-scale__panels">
    <div class="research-scale__panel" id="research-scale-panel-asteroid" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="research-scale-tab-asteroid" tabindex="0">
      <figure class="research-scale__figure">
        <div class="research-scale__media">
          <img src="/assets/img/research-scale/bennu-whole.png" width="1024" height="968" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Whole-body mosaic of asteroid Bennu, with a rough, boulder-covered surface against black space, photographed by OSIRIS-REx." />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="research-scale__caption">
          <p class="research-scale__eyebrow">Bennu / whole-body view</p>
          <h3>The geological setting</h3>
          <p>OSIRIS-REx observed Bennu before returning samples to Earth. The asteroid view establishes the setting; laboratory observations reveal the mineral record.</p>
          <dl class="research-scale__facts">
            <div><dt>Observation</dt><dd>Spacecraft imaging</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Research question</dt><dd>How do minerals record an asteroid's history?</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="research-scale__credit">Image: NASA/Goddard/University of Arizona.<br /><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/osiris-rex-debuts-new-bennu-mosaic-3d-shape-model/">NASA source</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/nasa-brand-center/images-and-media/">Media use</a></p>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="research-scale__panel" id="research-scale-panel-sample" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="research-scale-tab-sample" tabindex="0">
      <figure class="research-scale__figure">
        <div class="research-scale__media">
          <img src="/assets/img/research-scale/bennu-sample.jpg" width="1024" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Top-down photograph of the opened OSIRIS-REx sample collector, with dark Bennu rocks and dust inside." />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="research-scale__caption">
          <p class="research-scale__eyebrow">Bennu / returned material</p>
          <h3>A sample in the laboratory</h3>
          <p>The opened collector holds rocks and dust from Bennu. Returned material connects remote observations with measurements of individual particles.</p>
          <dl class="research-scale__facts">
            <div><dt>Observation</dt><dd>Sample photography</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Research question</dt><dd>Which particles preserve the textures of interest?</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="research-scale__credit">Image: NASA/Erika Blumenfeld &amp; Joseph Aebersold.<br /><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-detail/osiris-rex-pe-examination-5/">NASA source</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/nasa-brand-center/images-and-media/">Media use</a></p>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="research-scale__panel" id="research-scale-panel-microstructure" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="research-scale-tab-microstructure" tabindex="0">
      <figure class="research-scale__figure">
        <div class="research-scale__media">
          <img src="/assets/img/research-scale/bennu-microstructure.jpg" width="1536" height="1120" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Published four-panel Bennu figure: an optical view of a phosphate-bearing particle and three scanning electron microscope views of a fragment, with original scale bars." />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="research-scale__caption">
          <p class="research-scale__eyebrow">Bennu / grain and microstructure</p>
          <h3>Minerals in context</h3>
          <p>A published Bennu particle and SEM views of its fragment reveal phosphate-bearing textures. This is a comparison of observation scales, not evidence for dolomite in Orgueil.</p>
          <dl class="research-scale__facts">
            <div><dt>Observation</dt><dd>Optical microscopy + SEM</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Research question</dt><dd>How do texture, chemistry and crystal structure connect?</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="research-scale__credit">Figure: Lauretta &amp; Connolly et al. (2024), unchanged NASA rendition.<br /><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/missions/osiris-rex/surprising-phosphate-finding-in-nasas-osiris-rex-asteroid-sample/">NASA source</a> &middot; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/maps.14227">Paper</a> &middot; <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a></p>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="research-scale__boundary">These are public Bennu observations, not my Orgueil results. Bennu is not identified as Orgueil's parent body.</p>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>
